## 0. AGENT OPERATING RULES
0.1 Work strictly phase-by-phase (see §13). Never implement a later phase's features early; where an interface is needed, stub it with a docstring naming the responsible phase.
0.2 Before coding any phase: restate your understanding in exactly 5 bullets. Ask no questions; resolve ambiguity by choosing the simplest option consistent with this document and record it in PHASE_NOTES.md.
0.3 After each phase: run every acceptance command yourself, show verbatim output, then commit with the exact commit message given. Never claim success without shown output.
0.4 Never weaken a Safety Invariant (§9). If two requirements conflict, fail closed and note it in PHASE_NOTES.md.
0.5 End every phase response with: files created/changed, decisions recorded, acceptance outputs, deviations (should be none).

## 1. IDENTITY & PHILOSOPHY
1.1 NAME: SafeShell — a verified transactional command execution framework for Linux.
1.2 TAGLINE: "AI proposes. Policy restricts. Simulation verifies. Snapshots protect. Audit records everything. And the system learns."
1.3 CORE DECISION: AI proposes, but never decides. Every AI draft is schema-validated, policy-checked, and simulation-tested before trust. Safety comes from the transactional architecture, not from confidence in a language model.
1.4 AUDIENCES: human admins (undo button), DevOps pipelines (dry-run guarantees, <100 ms known-command overhead), AI agents (safety gateway; never direct shell).

## 2. PROBLEM STATEMENT & EVALUATION CRITERIA
2.1 PROBLEM: "Design a secure command execution framework that generates AI-based rollback plans, simulates command execution, and provides recovery mechanisms to safely execute system operations. Linux-based solutions only."
2.2 The submission is judged on: Novelty & Innovation; Technical Feasibility & Architecture; AI/Technical Approach (incl. justification of open-source/in-house models); Implementation Quality; Problem Alignment; Documentation Quality; Security; Scalability; Datasets Used; User Experience. When choosing between two implementations, prefer the one that scores more rubric criteria, and make the mapping explicit in docs/RUBRIC_MAPPING.md.

## 3. ENVIRONMENT CONSTRAINTS
3.1 TARGET OS: Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, kernel >= 6.8. LINUX-ONLY primitives allowed/expected: unshare(2) namespaces, OverlayFS, eBPF tracepoints, cgroups v2, systemd. Never use non-Linux APIs. (System python on 24.04 is 3.12; that satisfies ">=3.11".)
3.2 REFERENCE HARDWARE (design for exactly this): i5-13420H, 16 GB RAM, RTX 2050 with 4 GB VRAM, NVMe SSD.
3.3 MODEL RUNTIME: Ollama at http://localhost:11434/v1. Planner model = qwen2.5-coder:3b (Q4), exposed as "safeshell-planner-3b" via Modfile (PARAMETER temperature 0.1, PARAMETER num_ctx 2048, SYSTEM prompt per §9.5). Fallback/edge/fine-tune base = qwen2.5-coder:1.5b. Models are LAZY-LOADED (never at startup); OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=5m in prod mode, 24h for demos.
3.4 NO network egress anywhere in application code except localhost:11434. A hosted-model adapter may exist but is opt-in and ledger-logged.

## 4. ARCHITECTURE
4.1 COMPONENTS (name — role — implementation):
- CLI/TUI gateway — entrypoint, approval dashboard, plain-English explanations — Python Typer + Rich.
- Command parser — executable/flags/resolved paths/pipes/redirects/priv-esc; scripts → effect graph — shlex (MVP), tree-sitter-bash (roadmap).
- Risk classifier — tier Low/Medium/High/Critical — deterministic rules + XGBoost (CPU, <5 ms, 0 VRAM).
- Blast Radius scorer — quantitative BRS from live state (§4.6).
- Trusted state collector — ground-truth sha256 hashes, perms, ownership, service status — Rust core.
- Template store / RAG advisor — deterministic undo tables + few-shot retrieval; ADVISORY ONLY; grows via learning loop — SQLite FTS5.
- AI rollback planner — N-sample schema-constrained JSON plans — Ollama 3B (Instructor/grammar).
- Causal undo planner — dependency-ordered reversal, effect subsumption, consumption edges — Python graph module.
- Deterministic validator — schema/closed-vocab/path-policy/snapshot-precondition/ed25519 signature.
- Policy engine — policy-as-code approval (safe AST-whitelist evaluator, NO eval()) — YAML.
- Simulation engine — sandbox runs command AND rollback; post-rollback hash must equal pre-hash; judges N candidates; degradation tiers — Rust: unshare + OverlayFS + eBPF (aya).
- Transactional executor — real run under cgroup limits + live eBPF trace vs prediction.
- Recovery engine — L1 causal undo → L2 snapshot restore → L3 manual console.
- Counterfactual replay engine — re-simulate past txns / what-if futures against current state.
- Learning loop — dual-gated writeback of verified+committed plans into template store; auto-quarantine after 2 failures.
- Ledger anomaly detector (STRETCH) — isolation forest over per-user ledger features.
- Audit ledger — hash-chained append-only JSON (entry_hash = sha256(prev_hash + canonical_json(payload))) + SQLite index.
4.2 TRANSACTION LIFECYCLE (14 steps): parse → classify → score BRS → collect live state → plan (template → RAG → N-sample LLM) → causal order → validate(+signature) → simulate (best-of-N judged) → approve (TUI or policy-as-code) → execute+monitor → verify(+TOCTOU re-hash) → commit/rollback → journal+learn → expose undo & replay.
4.3 INFERENCE CASCADE (GPU on-demand; >90% of commands never touch GPU):
```python
def make_plan(cmd):
    p = parse(cmd)                               # CPU <10ms, effect graph
    if classify(p) == "critical" or (b := brs(p, live_state())) > POLICY.max:
        return DENY                              # fail-closed hard ceiling
    if (t := template_lookup(p)): return validate(t)          # <5ms, no AI
    r = rag_retrieve(p)                          # FTS5 <20ms
    if r.exact_match: return validate(instantiate(r, p))      # no AI
    n = 3 if tier(p) == "high" else 1
    return simulation_select(llm_plan_n(p, few_shot=r.top3, n=n), p)
```
4.4 SIMULATION-AS-JUDGE:
```python
def simulation_select(candidates, parsed):
    for plan in ranked_by_confidence(candidates):
        rep = sandbox.run(parsed.command, then=plan)
        if rep.post_rollback_hash == rep.pre_hash:
            plan.source = "ai_generated_sim_selected"; return plan
    return escalate("no candidate verified — fail closed")
```
4.5 CAUSAL UNDO: rollback = reversed topological sort over the effect graph with subsumption (a snapshot restore dedups the mkdir/cp it already undoes) and consumption edges (external effects → degradation-tier flag, never silent). Property to test: undo(undo(G)) ≡ identity.
4.6 BRS (config/brs.yaml, versioned, documented in docs/BRS.md):
BRS = Σ_paths(file_count × size_weight × irreversibility_weight) + Σ_services(dependents × 20) + network_externality(+200) + privilege(+150); weights: system paths(/etc,/boot,/usr,/var) ×5; no snapshot coverage ×3; recursive delete +2 per live file; wildcard >100 matches +100. TUI shows top-3 contributing signals. Uses: gating, simulation depth, anomaly baselines, approver communication.
4.7 DEGRADATION TIERS: T1 full verification (local file/metadata/service ops, hash-proven); T2 snapshot-only (network syscalls denied/stubbed via CLONE_NEWNET; undo best-effort); T3 blocked-with-explanation (external side effects, hardware ops) → deny or --accept-irreversible with elevated approval. Tiers are ALWAYS visible in TUI/reports.
4.8 POLICY-AS-CODE (config/policy.yaml; every auto-approval ledger-logs the firing rule):
```yaml
auto_approve: [{when: "tier == 'low' and brs < 100"},
               {when: "tier == 'medium' and brs < 500 and guarantee == 'T1'"}]
require_human: [{when: "brs >= 500 or guarantee == 'T2'"}]
deny: [{when: "tier == 'critical' or guarantee == 'T3'"}]
```

## 5. FEATURE SET & SCOPE GOVERNANCE
5.1 MVP (must build): single commands rm/mv/cp/touch/chmod/mkdir/ln; sequential script bundles as one atomic transaction; BRS; rules+XGBoost classifier; templates; FTS5 RAG; N=3 simulation-as-judge for High; ed25519-signed plans; policy-as-code; T1/T2/T3; 3-tier recovery; hash-chained ledger; Rich TUI approval; undo/replay/whatif; learning-loop writeback; selftest battery; benchmark harness; adversarial-corpus regression tests.
5.2 STRETCH (only if time permits): anomaly detector; systemctl in MVP set; QLoRA 1.5B weights (notebook + data-gen scripts ship regardless).
5.3 ROADMAP (document, do not build): network record/replay stub, tree-sitter-bash, RBAC, fleet/K8s, SIEM export.

